# ACMP backup schedule (P18b; cloud shape in P23). Install on the instance's deploy user:  crontab crontab.example
# Nightly full backup + every-4h business-day backups so data loss never exceeds 4h (NFR-056 RPO); RTO <= 8h
# (NFR-057) is met by restoring from the off-instance copy. backup.sh writes ONE .bak per stateful database
# (Acmp AND keycloak — ADR-0036 put Keycloak on the same SQL Server) into ACMP_BACKUP_DIR, then copies them to
# s3://$ACMP_BACKUP_BUCKET/sql/ via the instance profile (NFR-058: the copy must not share fate with the box).
# See deploy/runbooks/cloud-backup-dr.md.
#
# Adjust the path to your checkout. Output is appended to a log for after-the-fact detail.
#
# FAILURES ARE ALERTED BY backup.sh ITSELF, not by cron. This comment used to say a non-zero exit "is
# where an alert hooks in (mail, Seq webhook, etc.)" — a placeholder that was never filled, so a failed
# nightly backup was SILENT for as long as this file has existed. cron cannot help here: the lines below
# redirect stdout+stderr to a log, and Amazon Linux 2023 ships no MTA, so cron has nowhere to mail
# anything even without the redirect. backup.sh now publishes failures to ACMP_ALERT_TOPIC_ARN from an
# EXIT trap, and says so loudly in the log if that variable is unset or the publish is refused.

# ACMP_ENV_FILE IS LOAD-BEARING (DEF-022). Without it backup.sh defaults to deploy/.env, which exists
# on-prem but NOT in cloud — the bootstrap writes deploy/.env.cloud. The miss used to be swallowed, so
# ACMP_BACKUP_BUCKET stayed unset and the S3 copy was skipped while the run still reported success.
# backup.sh now refuses instead of falling through, but set it here anyway: the refusal is the net,
# not the plan. On-prem, point this at /opt/acmp/deploy/.env.
ACMP_ENV_FILE=/opt/acmp/deploy/.env.cloud

# Nightly full backup at 02:00
0 2 * * *      cd /opt/acmp && deploy/scripts/backup.sh >> /var/log/acmp-backup.log 2>&1

# Every 4 hours during business days (Sun-Thu 08:00, 12:00, 16:00, 20:00) to hold RPO <= 4h
0 8,12,16,20 * * 0-4   cd /opt/acmp && deploy/scripts/backup.sh >> /var/log/acmp-backup.log 2>&1
